{{smaller block|Now let the two parts while superposed be ''translated'' to any other position, then the piece B may be slid off and back to its original position.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XV. 660.}}}}

'''2.''' To transfer from one office or charge to another. {{smaller|In ''eccles. law'': (''a'') To remove from one see to another: said of a bishop.}}

{{smaller block|At home, at this time, died John Peers, Archbishop of York, in whose place succeeded Matthew Hatton, ''translated'' from the See of Durham.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 381.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') In Scottish Presbyterian churches, to transfer from one pastoral charge to another: said of a clergyman.}}

'''3.''' To remove or convey to heaven without death.

{{smaller block|By faith Enoch was ''translated'' that he should not see death.
{{right|Heb. xi. 5.}}}}

'''4†.''' To put into an ecstasy; ravish; put out of or beside one's self.

{{smaller block|He [St. Paul] was ''translated'' out of himself to behold it [Heaven]; but being returned into himself could not express it.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, i. 49.}}}}

'''5†.''' To cause to remove from one part of the body to another: as, to ''translate'' a disease.—'''6.''' To change into another form; transform.

{{smaller block|Unnethe the peple hir knew for hir fairnesse,
Whan she ''translated'' was in swich richesse.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Clerk's Tale, l. 329.}}}}

{{smaller block|Re-enter Puck, and Bottom with an ass's head. . . .
''Quince.'' Bless thee, Bottom! bless thee! thou art ''translated.''
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iii. 1. 122.}}}}

{{smaller block|Poets that can men into stars ''translate'',
And hurle men downe under the feete of Fate.
{{right|''Brome'', Sparagus Garden, iii. 5.}}}}

'''7.''' To render into another language; express the sense of (something expressed in the words of one language) in the words of another language; interpret.

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|And ȝee schulle undirstonde, that I have put this boke out of Latyn into Frensche, and ''translated'' it aȝen out of Frensche into Englyssche, that every man of my nacioun may undirstonde it. {{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. xi.}}}}

{{smaller block|Neither of the rivals [Pope and Tickell] can be said to have ''translated'' the "Iliad," unless, indeed, the word translation be used in the sense which it bears in the "Midsummer Night's Dream." {{right|''Macaulay'', Addison.}}}}

8. To explain by using other words; express in other terms; hence, figuratively, to present in another form.

{{smaller block|''Translating'' into his own clear, pure, and flowing language what he found in books well known to the world, but too bulky or too dry for boys and girls. {{right|''Macaulay'', Oliver Goldsmith.}}}}

{{smaller block|There is a magnificent series of stalls, which are simply the intricate embroidery of the tombs ''translated'' into polished oak. {{right|''H. James, Jr.'', Little Tour, p. 247.}}}}

9. To make clear or evident to the mind or to the senses without speech; convey to the mind or the senses, as by experience.—10. To manufacture from old material; especially, in cheap shoemaking, to make (shoes or boots) by using parts of old ones. [Slang.]

{{smaller block|Among these things are blankets, . . . ''translated'' boots, mended trowsers. {{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, II. 110.}}}}

11. In ''teleg.'', to retransmit (a message). See ''translation'', 7. ={{smaller|'''Syn.''' 7 and 8. ''Render, Interpret, Translate, Construe. Render'' is the most general in its meaning, but is usually followed by ''into:'' as, to ''render'' Gray's}}

