{{smaller block|Made and done was the ''translacion'' [to Paris] . . .
Off hed and of the glorious body [of St. Louis].
{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 6206.}}}}

{{smaller block|The solemn ''translation'' of St. Elphege's body from London to Canterbury is taken especial notice of in the Saxon Chronicle under the year 1023.
{{right|''Rock'', Church of our Fathers, III. i. 352, note.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') The removal of a person from one office to another, or from one sphere of duty to another; specifically, the removal of a bishop from one see to another; in Scotland, the removal of a clergyman from one pastoral charge to another.}}

{{smaller block|Does it follow that a law for keeping judges independent of the crown by preventing their ''translation'' is absolutely superfluous? {{right|''Brougham.''}}}}

{{smaller block|We can quite understand . . . Richard I. meditating the ''translation'' of the Archbishop of Monreale to Canterbury. {{right|''Stubbs'', Medieval and Modern Hist., p. 134.}}}}

{{smaller|(''c'') The removal of a person to heaven without death.}}

{{smaller block|Time, experience, self-reflections, and God's mercies make in some well-tempered minds a kind of ''translation'' before death. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Christ. Mor., ii. 6.}}}}

{{smaller|(''d'') The act of turning into another language; interpretation.}}

{{smaller block|The chiefest of his [King Athelstan's] Works for the Service of God and Good of his Subjects was the ''Translation'' of the Bible into the Saxon Tongue.
{{right|''Baker'', Chronicles, p. 10.}}}}

{{smaller block|At best, the ''translation'' of poetry is but an imitation of natural flowers in cambric or wax.
{{right|''Lowell'', Study Windows, p. 324.}}}}

2. That which is produced by turning into another language; a version; the reproduction of a literary composition in a language foreign to that of the original.

readings.c3s3:
{{smaller block|The English ''Translation'' of the Bible is the best ''Translation'' in the World. {{right|''Selden'', Table-Talk, p. 20.}}}}

¶
3†. In ''rhet.'', transference of the meaning of a word or phrase; metaphor.

{{smaller block|Metaphors, far-fet, hinder to be understood; and, affected, lose their grace; or when the person fetcheth his ''translations'' from a wrong place. {{right|''B. Jonson.''}}}}

4. In ''med.'', a change in the seat of a disease; metastasis.

{{smaller block|His disease was an asthma; the cause a metastasis or ''translation'' of humours from his joints to his lungs. {{right|''Harvey.''}}}}

5. The process of manufacturing from old material. [Slang.]

{{smaller block|''Translation'', as I understand it (said my informant), is this—to take a worn old pair of shoes or boots, and by repairing them make them appear as if left off with hardly any wear, as if they were only soiled. {{right|''Mayhew'', London Labour and London Poor, II. 40.}}}}

6. In ''mech.'', motion in which there is no rotation; rotation round an infinitely distant axis.

{{smaller block|A change of place in which there is no rotation is called a ''translation.'' In a rotation the different parts of the body are moving in different ways, but in a ''translation'' all parts move in the same way. {{right|''W. K. Clifford'', Lectures, II. 12.}}}}

